// expect: 42
//
// tests/64_large_hex_literal.c0 - a hex literal with the high bit set keeps its exact bit pattern
// Copyright (C) 2026 tavro. Part of coff, GPL-3.0-or-later (see ../LICENSE).
// Regression test: a hex literal with the high bit set must keep its
// exact bit pattern, not get clamped to LONG_MAX. coff0.c's lexer used
// strtol, which clamps any digit sequence that does not fit a signed
// long to 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF with ERANGE instead of preserving the
// bits. I hit this with a real value, a packed 8x8 font glyph bitmap
// (0xF88484F88484F800) that silently came back wrong. Fixed with
// strtoul, which parses the same digits as an unsigned magnitude; the
// cast back to long just reinterprets the bits. Same fix on the decimal
// path, for the same reason. The c0-written lexers never had this bug:
// their `v = v*16 + digit` accumulation wraps naturally in the
// register. They still get run over this file, since it is part of
// the same differential corpus as every other test.
//
// Checked with real arithmetic, not just "did it compile": the hex
// literal must equal an independently expressed value built from
// decimal plus unary minus, at a magnitude safely under the boundary
// either parsing function would hit, so the comparison does not depend
// on the same code path being right on both sides.
int main() {
    int hex_lit = 0xF88484F88484F800;
    int expected = 0 - 539159852467554304;
    if (hex_lit == expected) {
        return 42;
    }
    return 0;
}
